[~accountid:602f9aeb871d30006843505c] please find below in red the answers to the follow up questions we raised this morning:

Dear Samuel and Jose, 

 

We would like to ask some follow-up questions if I may: 

 

# Regarding your response to our first question, we understand that the intermediation in VTC activities is not subject to any regulatory requirement in terms of licenses or authorisations. However, we would like to confirm that the fact that we submit the information on behalf of the VTC license does not put us at risk of being reclassified as transport providers. Could you please assure us that submitting the information would not imply a reclassification as a transportation provider? {color:#ff5630}In our opinion, there should not be a reclassification. The mere submission of data is not a transport service.{color}
# Regarding the data privacy aspect, we will provide you with our Data Privacy Policy later for an in-depth discussion, but we suggest to wait until we learn more details about the process. {color:#ff5630}OK{color}
# Regarding your answer to our last question, I anticipate that your suggestions for a system whereby VTC license holders validate the information to be submitted before Bolt sends it out is difficult to implement as the information must be submitted immediately after the ride. However, we will discuss this with our business team.{color:#ff5630} Fully understood that this would be the ideal scenario to try to mitigate claims from VTC license holders but difficult to implement from an operational perspective.{color} As for your recommendation of subscribing an agreement with fleets/drivers to try, to          the maximum extent possible, to hold Bolt harmless, we understand that you talk        about an agreement to exclude our liability to VTC license holders. Is this something      that can be added to the Fleet partners agreements and/or T&Cs instead of rather          than entering into a separate agreement for that purpose? {color:#ff5630}Sure, this is something that can be included in the existing agreements/T&Cs to try to limit the liability to the maximum extent according to the applicable law -e.g. wilful misconduct / gross negligence cannot be limited/excluded in case Spanish law is applicable-. In any event, the enforceability of the provisions may also depend on the manner they are accepted / capacity of negotiation of the counterpart, as the VTC license holders/fleet partners might construe that these are adhesion contracts. {color}

We will schedule a call to discuss further next 9th December.

In the meantime, if you have any questions, do let us know.



Thank you.

