Hello [~accountid:61445ecbe057c6006a5163cc] - the feedback is as follows:

§      in the event of a change in the use of a building or its part without the required notification, the construction supervision authority shall issue a decision suspending the use of the building (or its part) and imposing the obligation to present certain documents specified in the Construction Law;

§      subsequently the construction supervision authority checks the fulfilment of the imposed obligations:

* if the obligations imposed by the construction supervision authority have been met, a legalization fee is imposed;

* if the obligations imposed by the construction supervision authority have +not+ been met then the authority may: (i) prohibit further use of the premises and (ii) order the premises to be restored to their original condition;

§      regardless of the above:

* a change in the manner of use of a building (or its part) without the required notification or despite an objection raised is subject to a fine (in the amount of PLN 20 to PLN 5,000) and is an offense;

* failure to comply with administrative decisions issued by the construction supervision authorities (despite the use of enforcement measures) is subject to a penalty of arrest, restriction of liberty or a fine.

Having pushed the externals a little further on the likelihood of the risk, the feedback was that the construction supervision authority very rarely initiate (ex officio) proceedings for unauthorized change of use of the premises. However, if they do so, most often they also issue a decision suspending the use of the premises. In practice, the initiation of such proceedings is usually associated with: (i) denunciation from neighbours (e.g. dissatisfied because of noise) or (ii) denunciation from competitors.

 Having queried also about the process itself, I understand that in the proceedings related to the change of the use of the premises the construction supervision authority have generally +30 days+ to submit an objection against the motion of the investor. This deadline applies if the motion of the investor is complete and factually correct. Thus, if there are any documents / information missing, the construction supervision authority is obliged to call the investor for such missing documents and the deadline is extended. I appreciate that it will  depend on the practice of each construction supervision authority - but in general, if the motion is correct in all aspects, the authority has 30 days to submit an objection. Does the concern about a worst case scenario of a 12 month wait come from a known practice of a particular authority, even when the motion is complete and accurate? 

Please let me know if you should have any further queries in connection with this? 

Many thanks, 

Liisa

